import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B6nQ8gjX.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Two-factor login","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/two-factor.md","filePath":"guide/two-factor.md","lastUpdated":1793710635000}`),a={name:`guide/two-factor.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="two-factor-login" tabindex="-1">Two-factor login <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-factor-login" aria-label="Permalink to “Two-factor login”">​</a></h1><p>By default the hub&#39;s login asks for one thing, the operator password. With <code>TWO_FACTOR=totp</code> it asks for a second one as well: the six-digit code an authenticator app shows for the hub (TOTP, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RFC 6238</a>). Every connector you add, in any client, sends you through that login, so a password that leaked — typed into the wrong window, pasted into a chat, read off a screen — no longer approves a client on its own.</p><p>The same factors unlock <a href="/guide/unlock">locked servers</a>: with two-factor login on, the unlock page asks for the code as well.</p><p>Two-factor login is off unless you turn it on. Nothing changes for a hub that does not set the variable.</p><h2 id="turning-it-on" tabindex="-1">Turning it on <a class="header-anchor" href="#turning-it-on" aria-label="Permalink to “Turning it on”">​</a></h2><p><strong>1. Make a secret and put it into your authenticator app.</strong> Run the admin CLI interactively inside the container:</p><div class="language-sh" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light-default github-dark-default" style="--shiki-light:#1f2328;--shiki-dark:#e6edf3;--shiki-light-bg:#ffffff;--shiki-dark-bg:#0d1117;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#953800;--shiki-dark:#FFA657;">docker</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> exec</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0550AE;--shiki-dark:#79C0FF;"> -it</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> mcp-hub</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> /app/dist/admin.js</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> totp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;"> init</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It prints a QR code into the terminal, the same secret as a setup key in groups of four, an <code>otpauth://</code> link and an <code>apple-otpauth://</code> link. Scan the code or type the key into any authenticator app. On an iPhone the Passwords app takes the key under <em>Set Up Verification Code</em>, and the second link opens it there directly. Then type the code the app shows. The command prints the two lines for the hub&#39;s environment only once that code matched, so a mistyped key or a phone whose clock is off is caught here and not at your next login.</p><div class="language-txt" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">txt</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light-default github-dark-default" style="--shiki-light:#1f2328;--shiki-dark:#e6edf3;--shiki-light-bg:#ffffff;--shiki-dark-bg:#0d1117;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>TWO_FACTOR=totp</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>TOTP_SECRET=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXPJBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>--light</code> draws the QR code for a light terminal background, <code>--label &lt;name&gt;</code> changes the name the app lists the hub under (the host of <code>EXTERNAL_URL</code> by default), and <code>--no-confirm</code> skips the check for scripts.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">The output contains the secret</p><p>Anything that records terminal output keeps it: shell history tools that capture output, <code>script</code>, CI logs. Clear it there, and keep the secret out of the password manager that holds the hub password. Whoever opens a vault holding both has both factors.</p></div><p><strong>2. Hand the two lines to the hub.</strong> In Compose, with the secret in <code>.env</code>:</p><div class="language-yaml" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">yaml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light-default github-dark-default" style="--shiki-light:#1f2328;--shiki-dark:#e6edf3;--shiki-light-bg:#ffffff;--shiki-dark-bg:#0d1117;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#116329;--shiki-dark:#7EE787;">environment</span><span style="--shiki-light:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#E6EDF3;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#116329;--shiki-dark:#7EE787;">  TWO_FACTOR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#E6EDF3;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;">&quot;totp&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#116329;--shiki-dark:#7EE787;">  TOTP_SECRET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#1F2328;--shiki-dark:#E6EDF3;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#0A3069;--shiki-dark:#A5D6FF;">&quot;\${TOTP_SECRET}&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>3. Restart.</strong> The log says <code>two-factor login is on</code>, and, the first time, <code>two-factor login was switched on since the last start</code>.</p><h2 id="what-your-connectors-notice" tabindex="-1">What your connectors notice <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-your-connectors-notice" aria-label="Permalink to “What your connectors notice”">​</a></h2><p><strong>They sign in once more.</strong> While two-factor login is on, the hub refuses what a sign-in without a code had issued:</p><ul><li><strong>Refresh tokens and authorization codes</strong> from such a sign-in are refused with <code>invalid_grant</code>, and the client sends you to the login page again. A refreshed token inherits what its first sign-in proved, so a connector that signed in with a code keeps refreshing.</li><li><strong>A browser session</strong> from such a sign-in no longer skips the login page.</li><li><strong>The hub&#39;s session cookie</strong>, which the consent page and the <a href="/guide/configuration#upstreams-that-speak-oauth">upstream OAuth callback</a> check, is signed together with the mode. Switching the mode in either direction ends it.</li><li><strong>Access tokens</strong> that are already out keep working until they expire, which is fifteen minutes at most.</li></ul><p><strong>Admin API tokens are not affected.</strong> Tokens from <a href="/reference/admin-cli#tokens"><code>mcp-hub-admin tokens create</code></a> never pass through the login page, so there is no code to ask for. They are bearer credentials for machines. Keep them short-lived, bound to one resource, and revoke the ones you no longer use.</p><p>Switching two-factor login off again is the same edit in reverse. The password alone signs in, and tokens that were issued with a code keep working.</p><h2 id="the-login-page" tabindex="-1">The login page <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-login-page" aria-label="Permalink to “The login page”">​</a></h2><p>The code field sits under the password field and carries <code>autocomplete=&quot;one-time-code&quot;</code> and <code>inputmode=&quot;numeric&quot;</code>. A phone therefore offers the current code from its password manager or Passwords app, and opens the number keyboard otherwise. Spaces are ignored, so <code>123 456</code> works.</p><p>A wrong password and a wrong code get the same answer, <em>Wrong password or code</em>. The page does not tell anyone who is guessing which half was right.</p><h2 id="codes-mistakes-and-pauses" tabindex="-1">Codes, mistakes and pauses <a class="header-anchor" href="#codes-mistakes-and-pauses" aria-label="Permalink to “Codes, mistakes and pauses”">​</a></h2><p><strong>A code works once.</strong> The hub accepts the code for the current 30-second step and one step either side, to allow for clocks that drift and fingers that are slow. Once a step was used, it and every earlier one are refused: <em>This code was already used. Wait for the next one.</em> The last used step is stored in <code>state.json</code>, so the hub and the admin CLI agree on it.</p><p><strong>Wrong passwords</strong> count against the per-address login limit, ten per fifteen minutes, and log <code>authentication failure from &lt;ip&gt;</code>. That is the line the documented <a href="/guide/deployment#fail2ban">fail2ban filter</a> bans on.</p><p><strong>Wrong codes after a correct password</strong> pause sign-in, from the fifth one on: thirty seconds, then doubling with every further wrong code, up to an hour. The page answers <code>429</code> with a <code>Retry-After</code>, and a correct code resets the count. These attempts log <code>second-factor failure from &lt;ip&gt;</code>, which the filter does <strong>not</strong> match, so a typo on your own phone does not ban your own address. Only someone who knows the password can start the pause. While it lasts, a wrong password gets the same <code>429</code> as a right one, so the pause does not confirm a guess either.</p><h2 id="when-the-secret-is-missing-or-broken" tabindex="-1">When the secret is missing or broken <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-the-secret-is-missing-or-broken" aria-label="Permalink to “When the secret is missing or broken”">​</a></h2><p>With <code>TWO_FACTOR=totp</code> and a <code>TOTP_SECRET</code> that is unset, not Base32 or shorter than 160 bits, nobody can sign in. The hub still starts and serves its discovery documents, the first log line says what is wrong, and the login page answers <code>503</code> with the reason. It never falls back to the password alone. An unknown <code>TWO_FACTOR</code> value stops the hub at startup.</p><h2 id="a-lost-phone" tabindex="-1">A lost phone <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-lost-phone" aria-label="Permalink to “A lost phone”">​</a></h2><p>Recovery happens on the host, by whoever can change the hub&#39;s environment. Either set <code>TWO_FACTOR=off</code>, sign in with the password and set up a new authenticator, or run <code>totp init</code> again and replace <code>TOTP_SECRET</code>. There are no recovery codes: anyone who can do either already controls the hub, and nobody else should be able to.</p><h2 id="what-it-does-not-protect-against" tabindex="-1">What it does not protect against <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-does-not-protect-against" aria-label="Permalink to “What it does not protect against”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Phishing.</strong> A code typed into a convincing copy of the login page can be replayed by whoever runs the copy, within the code&#39;s 30 seconds. Look at the address before you type.</li><li><strong>A compromised stdio server.</strong> Per the <a href="/guide/security#trust-model">trust model</a> a child process runs as the hub&#39;s user and can read the hub&#39;s environment, <code>TOTP_SECRET</code> included. The second factor guards the login, not the container.</li></ul><h2 id="reference" tabindex="-1">Reference <a class="header-anchor" href="#reference" aria-label="Permalink to “Reference”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/reference/environment#two-factor-login"><code>TWO_FACTOR</code> and <code>TOTP_SECRET</code></a></li><li><a href="/reference/admin-cli#totp"><code>mcp-hub-admin totp init</code></a></li><li><a href="/guide/security">Security</a> — rate limits and the trust model</li></ul>`,34)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};